1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,035
Hello, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,035 --> 00:00:17,396
Tonight's story is a long, slow journey, and you are invited to come along simply as a passenger.

3
00:00:17,396 --> 00:00:23,467
You will not need to lead the way, or carry anything, or remember a single name.

4
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:25,288
The road will carry you.

5
00:00:25,288 --> 00:00:34,242
If you drift off somewhere along it, the camels will keep walking, and the story will keep going, softly, without you.

6
00:00:35,742 --> 00:00:39,822
Before we set out, let your shoulders drop a little.

7
00:00:39,822 --> 00:00:42,646
Let your hands grow heavy and still.

8
00:00:42,646 --> 00:00:49,863
Breathe in slowly, and then let the breath go, as slowly as sand slipping from an open palm.

9
00:00:49,863 --> 00:00:52,766
There is nowhere else you need to be.

10
00:00:54,266 --> 00:01:07,909
We are going back about twelve hundred years, to the middle of the eighth century, to the western edge of China, where the green land runs out and the great deserts of Central Asia begin.

11
00:01:07,909 --> 00:01:09,295
It is early autumn.

12
00:01:09,295 --> 00:01:14,256
The worst heat of summer is over, and the nights are clear and cool.

13
00:01:14,256 --> 00:01:23,813
A caravan of camels is about to leave an oasis town and walk west, by moonlight, along one of the oldest trade routes in the world.

14
00:01:25,313 --> 00:01:33,500
We will travel with it for one whole night, from the last light of evening to the first grey hint of dawn.

15
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:39,525
We will listen to the bells, and watch the stars turn, and rest beside a well.

16
00:01:39,525 --> 00:01:43,233
And by the end, I hope, you will be resting too.

17
00:01:52,733 --> 00:02:03,135
Our journey begins in Dunhuang, a walled town in a long, green oasis, fed by a river that comes down from the snowy mountains to the south.

18
00:02:03,135 --> 00:02:06,952
All around it, in every direction, there is desert.

19
00:02:06,952 --> 00:02:21,021
But here, along the water channels, there are orchards of apricot and pear, fields of millet and wheat, and long, straight rows of poplar trees, their leaves just beginning to turn yellow.

20
00:02:22,521 --> 00:02:31,602
The name Dunhuang is often translated as something like blazing beacon, or great and flourishing, and both meanings fit.

21
00:02:31,602 --> 00:02:52,942
For centuries it was the last real town at the western end of the Chinese world, the place where travellers bound for Central Asia took on their final supplies, and where travellers coming the other way, after weeks in the desert, saw green trees for the first time in a long while.

22
00:02:54,442 --> 00:02:56,386
Water is everything here.

23
00:02:56,386 --> 00:03:06,650
Small channels, lined with stones and edged with grass, carry the river through the oasis, from field to field and garden to garden.

24
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:13,803
Each family has its own hours for opening the little earthen gates and letting the water in.

25
00:03:13,803 --> 00:03:20,490
In the evening, you can hear it running, quiet and cool, along the edges of the lanes.

26
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:26,050
In the evening, the market streets are slowly emptying.

27
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:29,814
A seller of dried apricots is folding up his cloth.

28
00:03:29,814 --> 00:03:33,873
A potter is stacking the last of his bowls in a basket.

29
00:03:33,873 --> 00:03:53,137
From an open doorway comes the smell of flatbread baking against the inside wall of a clay oven, and the soft sound of people talking in several different languages at once, Chinese, and Sogdian, and Turkic, and Khotanese, all mingling together in the cool air.

30
00:03:54,637 --> 00:04:01,878
Just south of the town, the sand rises into enormous dunes, some of them taller than a temple tower.

31
00:04:01,878 --> 00:04:05,208
People called them the Singing Sand Mountains.

32
00:04:05,208 --> 00:04:19,617
When the wind moves over their crests, or when the sand slides down their steep faces, they make a low, humming sound, like a distant drum, or the deepest string of an instrument, played very softly.

33
00:04:19,617 --> 00:04:24,034
Travellers wrote about that sound for many hundreds of years.

34
00:04:25,534 --> 00:04:30,892
And hidden among those dunes is something that seems almost impossible.

35
00:04:30,892 --> 00:04:34,212
A small spring, shaped like a crescent moon.

36
00:04:34,212 --> 00:04:42,437
Clear water, curving gently in the hollow between the sand hills, with reeds growing thickly along its edges.

37
00:04:42,437 --> 00:04:47,267
People have come to look at it for more than two thousand years.

38
00:04:47,267 --> 00:04:52,926
The dunes rise all around it, and yet, somehow, the sand never fills it in.

39
00:04:54,426 --> 00:04:57,755
This evening the spring is perfectly still.

40
00:04:57,755 --> 00:05:03,638
The reeds lean over the water, and their reflections lean down to meet them.

41
00:05:03,638 --> 00:05:15,482
The first stars are appearing above the dunes, and each one is doubled in the pool below, so that the little lake seems to hold a small piece of the sky.

42
00:05:16,982 --> 00:05:24,931
Some miles to the southeast of the town, in a long cliff above a dry riverbed, there are hundreds of caves.

43
00:05:24,931 --> 00:05:33,623
Monks and painters had been carving them out of the soft rock for nearly four hundred years by the time of our story.

44
00:05:33,623 --> 00:05:46,550
The story goes that a wandering monk, long ago, saw a golden light shining on the cliff, like a thousand glowing figures, and decided to make his meditation cave right there.

45
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:52,565
Inside those caves, every wall and every ceiling is painted.

46
00:05:52,565 --> 00:06:01,519
Musicians with lutes and flutes, gardens and palaces, flying figures with long ribbons trailing behind them like smoke.

47
00:06:01,519 --> 00:06:09,570
In the evening, a few small lamps glow in the cave mouths, where the monks are reading, or sitting quietly.

48
00:06:09,570 --> 00:06:17,170
Seen from the riverbed below, the lights look like a row of tiny windows in the side of the mountain.

49
00:06:18,670 --> 00:06:23,142
Now the bells of the town are sounding the close of day.

50
00:06:23,142 --> 00:06:25,378
The gates will soon be shut.

51
00:06:25,378 --> 00:06:36,478
But outside the western wall, in an open yard beside the road, lanterns are being lit, and there is a great deal of slow, careful activity.

52
00:06:36,478 --> 00:06:39,513
Our caravan is getting ready to leave.

53
00:06:49,013 --> 00:06:52,954
The yard smells of dust, and straw, and warm animals.

54
00:06:52,954 --> 00:07:04,629
Thirty or forty camels are kneeling in rows on the packed earth, their long legs folded neatly underneath them, their heads held high on their curving necks.

55
00:07:04,629 --> 00:07:17,941
They are Bactrian camels, the kind with two humps, shaggy and patient, perfectly suited to the deserts of Central Asia, where the summers are fierce and the winters bitterly cold.

56
00:07:19,441 --> 00:07:26,565
Each camel is fitted with a wooden frame that sits between its two humps, padded with felt.

57
00:07:26,565 --> 00:07:36,351
On that frame the loads are tied, balanced carefully on either side, so that the animal is never pulled one way or the other.

58
00:07:36,351 --> 00:07:45,510
A strong camel can carry well over two hundred kilograms, hour after hour, at a steady walking pace, for days on end.

59
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:50,809
The men loading them work slowly and without hurry.

60
00:07:50,809 --> 00:07:57,439
They lift a bundle, check its weight against its partner, adjust a strap, and lift again.

61
00:07:57,439 --> 00:08:03,101
The camels grumble a little, low throaty complaints, but they do not get up.

62
00:08:03,101 --> 00:08:10,029
They have done this many times before, and they know that the grumbling is simply part of it.

63
00:08:11,529 --> 00:08:13,202
What are they carrying?

64
00:08:13,202 --> 00:08:18,875
Bolts of silk, of course, wrapped tightly in plain cloth to keep out the dust.

65
00:08:18,875 --> 00:08:27,240
Silk was so valuable that it was often used as money, and soldiers at the frontier forts were sometimes paid in it.

66
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,495
But there are other things too.

67
00:08:29,495 --> 00:08:30,731
Bundles of paper.

68
00:08:30,731 --> 00:08:34,659
Small, carefully sealed packets of musk and medicines.

69
00:08:34,659 --> 00:08:40,478
Lacquered boxes, and bronze mirrors, and a few rolls of fine, patterned brocade.

70
00:08:41,978 --> 00:08:45,731
Some of the camels carry nothing for sale at all.

71
00:08:45,731 --> 00:08:49,330
Their loads are food and water for the journey.

72
00:08:49,330 --> 00:08:56,835
Sacks of millet and dried meat, bags of dried fruit, flat discs of bread that will keep for weeks.

73
00:08:56,835 --> 00:09:07,173
And on each side of the strongest animals, big leather water skins, sloshing gently, that will be refilled at every well along the way.

74
00:09:08,673 --> 00:09:13,188
Moving among them, checking every rope, is the caravan leader.

75
00:09:13,188 --> 00:09:25,570
In the language of the Sogdian merchants he would have had a title that the Chinese wrote as sabao, which came from an old word meaning simply, the leader of the caravan.

76
00:09:25,570 --> 00:09:37,878
He is a quiet, broad-shouldered man in a belted coat and soft boots, with a short beard and a felt hat, and he has made this journey more times than he can easily count.

77
00:09:39,378 --> 00:09:42,201
He stops beside each camel in turn.

78
00:09:42,201 --> 00:09:54,216
He runs his hand along its neck, looks at its feet, and says a few words to it, very softly, the way you might speak to a horse, or a sleeping child.

79
00:09:54,216 --> 00:09:57,764
The camels turn their heads to watch him go.

80
00:09:57,764 --> 00:09:59,538
They seem to know him.

81
00:10:01,038 --> 00:10:07,397
At the front of the line, a young boy is tying a bell around the neck of the lead camel.

82
00:10:07,397 --> 00:10:14,551
It is a deep bronze bell, about the size of a fist, and it makes a low, round sound with each step.

83
00:10:14,551 --> 00:10:18,959
A second, smaller bell hangs from the last camel in the line.

84
00:10:18,959 --> 00:10:29,582
That way, through the long dark night, the drivers at the front and the back can always hear each other, and know that no one has been left behind.

85
00:10:31,082 --> 00:10:34,867
Last of all, the travellers' own bags are loaded.

86
00:10:34,867 --> 00:10:37,261
Bedrolls of felt and sheepskin.

87
00:10:37,261 --> 00:10:38,575
A small iron pot.

88
00:10:38,575 --> 00:10:41,664
A lute in a cloth bag, carefully padded.

89
00:10:41,664 --> 00:10:48,616
The leader looks along the whole line one last time, from the front bell to the back bell.

90
00:10:48,616 --> 00:10:57,962
Then he nods, and the drivers call out, and one by one, with a great creaking and swaying, the camels rise to their feet.

91
00:11:07,462 --> 00:11:13,656
The caravan leaves the oasis just as the last light fades from the western sky.

92
00:11:13,656 --> 00:11:19,301
For a little while, the road runs between fields and irrigation ditches.

93
00:11:19,301 --> 00:11:25,024
Frogs are calling from the water, and the poplar leaves whisper overhead.

94
00:11:25,024 --> 00:11:30,277
Then the trees grow fewer, and the fields end, and the air changes.

95
00:11:30,277 --> 00:11:33,806
It becomes drier, and cooler, and very clear.

96
00:11:33,806 --> 00:11:35,217
The desert begins.

97
00:11:36,717 --> 00:11:38,188
Why travel at night?

98
00:11:38,188 --> 00:11:44,516
In the hot months, the desert by day could be dangerous, for animals and people alike.

99
00:11:44,516 --> 00:11:49,961
The sand grew too hot to walk on, and the sun drained everyone's strength.

100
00:11:49,961 --> 00:12:01,218
So caravans often rested through the heat of the day in whatever shade they could find, and walked in the cool hours, by the light of the moon and stars.

101
00:12:01,218 --> 00:12:06,221
Even now, in early autumn, the night is the gentlest time to travel.

102
00:12:07,721 --> 00:12:16,016
The camels settle into their natural pace, which is slow and unchanging, a little slower than a person walking.

103
00:12:16,016 --> 00:12:20,126
Their wide, soft feet make almost no sound on the sand.

104
00:12:20,126 --> 00:12:33,427
All you can hear is the creak of the wooden frames, the soft breathing of the animals, and the low bell at the front of the line, sounding again and again, like a slow heartbeat.

105
00:12:34,927 --> 00:12:43,341
Somewhere far ahead, many hours' walk to the northwest, on a low rise above the marshes, stands a square fortress.

106
00:12:43,341 --> 00:12:49,319
This is the Jade Gate, one of the great frontier passes of the old Chinese world.

107
00:12:49,319 --> 00:12:55,518
Its thick walls of rammed earth had been standing for more than eight hundred years.

108
00:12:55,518 --> 00:13:06,736
Its name, the story goes, came from the caravans of precious jade that passed through it on their way east, from the rivers of the far western kingdoms.

109
00:13:08,235 --> 00:13:15,625
In the time of our story, the main road had moved, and the old fort was quieter than it had once been.

110
00:13:15,625 --> 00:13:26,563
But its walls still rose against the sky, square and solid, and the travellers still spoke of it as the place where China ended and the far west began.

111
00:13:26,563 --> 00:13:38,444
A famous poem of the age says that even the spring wind never travels beyond the Jade Gate, as though the softness of spring stopped there, at the edge of the sand.

112
00:13:39,943 --> 00:13:44,599
Along the road, at regular intervals, stand old watchtowers.

113
00:13:44,599 --> 00:13:54,065
They are tall, square towers of earth and reeds, built in layers, one on top of another, and dried hard in the desert air.

114
00:13:54,065 --> 00:14:06,247
Long ago, signal fires were lit on top of them, one after another, so that a message could travel hundreds of miles in a single night, faster than any rider.

115
00:14:07,747 --> 00:14:10,082
Tonight, no fires are burning.

116
00:14:10,082 --> 00:14:18,252
The towers stand empty and silent, one after another, like tall, patient figures waiting beside the road.

117
00:14:18,252 --> 00:14:25,644
The caravan passes the first one, and then, after a long while, the second, and then the third.

118
00:14:25,644 --> 00:14:29,768
Each one looks almost exactly like the one before it.

119
00:14:29,768 --> 00:14:33,347
Counting them is a little like counting sheep.

120
00:14:34,847 --> 00:14:48,756
Before leaving China, every traveller needed a pass, a written permit from the officials, that listed who they were, and how many people and animals were with them, and where they were going.

121
00:14:48,756 --> 00:14:53,270
The caravan leader keeps his carefully folded inside his coat.

122
00:14:53,270 --> 00:15:02,154
At each checkpoint, a sleepy guard holds a lamp up to the paper, counts the camels, looks at the faces, and waves them on.

123
00:15:03,654 --> 00:15:07,142
And then the last checkpoint is behind them.

124
00:15:07,142 --> 00:15:14,515
The road ahead is only a faint track in the sand, marked here and there by a cairn of stones.

125
00:15:14,515 --> 00:15:19,827
The moon has risen now, large and pale, over the dunes to the east.

126
00:15:19,827 --> 00:15:28,785
It lays a soft silver light across everything, so bright that every camel walks beside its own long, slow shadow.

127
00:15:38,285 --> 00:15:40,267
Who are these travellers?

128
00:15:40,267 --> 00:15:44,310
Most of the merchants in this caravan are Sogdians.

129
00:15:44,310 --> 00:15:58,103
Their homeland lay far to the west, many weeks' journey away, in the green river valleys of what is now Uzbekistan and Tajikistan, around the cities of Samarkand and Bukhara.

130
00:15:58,103 --> 00:16:05,634
For several hundred years, the Sogdians were among the greatest traders of the whole Silk Road.

131
00:16:07,134 --> 00:16:08,927
They were famous for it.

132
00:16:08,927 --> 00:16:20,505
People said, half joking and half admiring, that Sogdian children were taught to speak sweetly and to hold their money tightly from the day they were born.

133
00:16:20,505 --> 00:16:34,398
They built small communities of their own in towns all along the road, all the way east to the Chinese capital, where they ran shops and inns and wine houses in the great western market.

134
00:16:35,898 --> 00:16:39,492
Something remarkable survives from these merchants.

135
00:16:39,492 --> 00:16:52,949
In a ruined watchtower not far from Dunhuang, an explorer in the early twentieth century found a small bundle of letters, written in the Sogdian language, more than fifteen hundred years old.

136
00:16:52,949 --> 00:16:59,079
They had been sent from China toward Samarkand, and somehow, they were never delivered.

137
00:16:59,079 --> 00:17:01,404
They are full of ordinary things.

138
00:17:01,404 --> 00:17:06,618
Prices, and debts, and news of family, and worries about friends far away.

139
00:17:08,118 --> 00:17:14,851
It is good to remember, too, that hardly anyone ever travelled the whole length of the Silk Road.

140
00:17:14,851 --> 00:17:17,073
The name itself is quite modern.

141
00:17:17,073 --> 00:17:21,446
It was coined in the nineteenth century by a German geographer.

142
00:17:21,446 --> 00:17:40,395
For the people who walked it, there was no single road at all, but a vast, shifting web of routes, from oasis to oasis, and most goods changed hands many times, passing from one merchant to the next, from one caravan to the next, like a story passed along a line of people.

143
00:17:41,895 --> 00:17:46,176
So our caravan is only travelling one part of the way.

144
00:17:46,176 --> 00:18:01,949
Perhaps as far as the next great oasis, at Turfan, or Kucha, where the silk will be sold, and other goods bought, and other camels loaded, and another caravan will carry it all a little further west.

145
00:18:03,449 --> 00:18:10,921
Riding near the middle of the line there is an older merchant, wrapped in a long, quilted coat.

146
00:18:10,921 --> 00:18:20,046
He has made the trip so many times that he dozes as he rides, his head nodding gently with the camel's rolling walk.

147
00:18:20,046 --> 00:18:29,091
Every so often he wakes, looks up at the stars to see how far the night has turned, and then closes his eyes again.

148
00:18:30,591 --> 00:18:34,908
Near him rides a younger man, on his first journey west.

149
00:18:34,908 --> 00:18:36,989
He is too excited to sleep.

150
00:18:36,989 --> 00:18:46,161
He keeps looking around at the moonlit dunes, at the long line of swaying camels ahead and behind, at the enormous sky.

151
00:18:46,161 --> 00:18:54,485
He is trying to remember all of it, so that one day, when he is old, he can describe it to his own children.

152
00:18:55,985 --> 00:19:08,730
Further back, walking beside the camels rather than riding, there is a Buddhist monk with a shaved head and a plain robe, carrying a staff and a small bundle of scrolls.

153
00:19:08,730 --> 00:19:18,535
Monks often travelled with caravans, for company and safety, on their way to study at the great monasteries in the oasis kingdoms.

154
00:19:18,535 --> 00:19:26,001
He walks in silence, and from time to time his lips move, softly, as he repeats a verse to himself.

155
00:19:27,501 --> 00:19:37,981
And so they go on through the night, the merchants and the drivers, the young traveller and the old one, the monk and the boy with the bells.

156
00:19:37,981 --> 00:19:41,921
Different languages, different gods, different homes.

157
00:19:41,921 --> 00:19:46,381
All of them walking the same slow road, under the same moon.

158
00:19:55,881 --> 00:19:57,901
It is past midnight now.

159
00:19:57,901 --> 00:20:04,214
The moon has climbed high, and the desert lies still and silver beneath it.

160
00:20:04,214 --> 00:20:16,083
The dunes rise and fall like slow, frozen waves, their sharp crests curving away into the distance, one after another, as far as you can see.

161
00:20:17,583 --> 00:20:20,811
Out here there is almost no sound at all.

162
00:20:20,811 --> 00:20:25,376
No birds, no insects, no running water, no wind in leaves.

163
00:20:25,376 --> 00:20:33,484
Only the soft footfalls of the camels, the creak of rope and wood, and the slow, deep note of the bell.

164
00:20:33,484 --> 00:20:41,434
The silence of the desert is so wide that the bell seems to go on sounding long after it has stopped.

165
00:20:42,934 --> 00:20:51,061
The air is dry and very clean, and without the glow of any town, the stars are astonishingly bright.

166
00:20:51,061 --> 00:20:57,806
They crowd the sky from one horizon to the other, thousands upon thousands of them.

167
00:20:57,806 --> 00:21:03,657
Across the middle of the sky runs the long, pale river of the Milky Way.

168
00:21:03,657 --> 00:21:08,940
In China, people called it the Silver River, the river of heaven.

169
00:21:10,439 --> 00:21:16,384
There is an old story about that river, told on summer and autumn nights all over China.

170
00:21:16,384 --> 00:21:21,585
On one side of it lives the Weaver Girl, a bright star who weaves the clouds.

171
00:21:21,585 --> 00:21:25,030
On the other side lives the Cowherd, who loves her.

172
00:21:25,030 --> 00:21:28,272
They are kept apart by the great river of stars.

173
00:21:28,272 --> 00:21:40,498
But once a year, the story goes, on the seventh night of the seventh month, all the magpies in the world fly up and make a bridge with their wings, so that the two of them can meet.

174
00:21:41,998 --> 00:21:44,827
The caravan leader does not need a map.

175
00:21:44,827 --> 00:21:48,888
He reads the sky the way you might read a familiar page.

176
00:21:48,888 --> 00:21:58,533
He knows which star stays fixed in the north while the others wheel slowly around it, and he keeps it always over his right shoulder.

177
00:21:58,533 --> 00:22:09,049
He knows which stars rise at which hour, and by watching them climb, he can tell how much of the night has passed, and how much still lies ahead.

178
00:22:10,549 --> 00:22:12,589
He also reads the ground.

179
00:22:12,589 --> 00:22:24,017
The shape of the dunes, the colour of the gravel, a line of stone cairns left by earlier travellers, a dry streambed, a particular low hill.

180
00:22:24,017 --> 00:22:34,954
Every landmark is a word in a long sentence that he learned by heart, many years ago, from the leader who walked this road before him.

181
00:22:36,454 --> 00:22:45,551
Sometimes the track crosses a stretch of hard, flat gravel, dark and smooth, scattered with small polished stones.

182
00:22:45,551 --> 00:22:57,681
Then it climbs again into soft sand, and the camels lean forward a little and slow down, and their feet sink and rise, sink and rise, without complaint.

183
00:22:59,181 --> 00:23:11,163
High on the face of a nearby dune, the sand shifts in the faint night breeze, and for a moment there is that sound again, the low hum of the singing sand.

184
00:23:11,163 --> 00:23:12,408
It is very soft.

185
00:23:12,408 --> 00:23:16,376
It could almost be someone humming in another room.

186
00:23:16,376 --> 00:23:21,901
The camels flick their ears toward it, and then forget it, and walk on.

187
00:23:23,401 --> 00:23:29,087
Once, far off, a small pale desert fox trots along the crest of a dune.

188
00:23:29,087 --> 00:23:37,576
It pauses, and looks down at the long line of camels passing below, its large ears turned toward the bell.

189
00:23:37,576 --> 00:23:42,941
Then it trots on, unhurried, and slips over the ridge, and is gone.

190
00:23:42,941 --> 00:23:49,107
There is only sand again, and starlight, and the slow, deep note of the bell.

191
00:23:50,607 --> 00:23:57,570
Somewhere near the back of the line, the young traveller has finally stopped looking around.

192
00:23:57,570 --> 00:23:59,160
His eyes are closing.

193
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:09,831
His body has found the rhythm of the camel's walk, the long, slow forward sway, and back again, and forward again, like a boat on a calm sea.

194
00:24:09,831 --> 00:24:15,205
He sleeps sitting up, the way the old merchants do, rocked by the road.

195
00:24:24,705 --> 00:24:31,381
In the small hours of the night, the leader lifts his hand, and the caravan begins to slow.

196
00:24:31,381 --> 00:24:41,286
Ahead, in a shallow hollow between the dunes, there is a darker patch on the sand, and a few low, scrubby bushes, and a ring of stones.

197
00:24:41,286 --> 00:24:42,240
It is a well.

198
00:24:42,240 --> 00:24:46,789
Wells like this were the beads on the string of the Silk Road.

199
00:24:46,789 --> 00:24:49,430
Every route was planned around them.

200
00:24:50,930 --> 00:24:55,816
The camels have smelled the water long before the people see it.

201
00:24:55,816 --> 00:25:02,992
Their heads come up, and their pace quickens a little, and the bell at the front rings faster.

202
00:25:02,992 --> 00:25:15,741
When they reach the hollow, the drivers call out a soft word, and one by one the great animals fold their legs, and kneel down on the sand with a long, contented sigh.

203
00:25:17,241 --> 00:25:20,327
A camel can go for days without drinking.

204
00:25:20,327 --> 00:25:24,542
But when it does drink, it drinks an astonishing amount.

205
00:25:24,542 --> 00:25:30,262
A thirsty camel can take in a hundred litres of water in just a few minutes.

206
00:25:30,262 --> 00:25:43,886
So the drivers draw up bucket after bucket from the well, on a long rope, and pour it into a wide leather trough, and the camels drink, and drink, with a slow, steady sucking sound.

207
00:25:45,386 --> 00:25:52,155
Then the water skins are filled, one after another, until they are round and heavy again.

208
00:25:52,155 --> 00:25:57,099
The well water is cold, and tastes faintly of minerals and stone.

209
00:25:57,099 --> 00:26:07,594
The drivers drink deeply too, and wash the dust from their faces, and pour a little over the backs of their necks, and sigh with pleasure.

210
00:26:09,094 --> 00:26:13,126
By the firelight you can see the camels more closely now.

211
00:26:13,126 --> 00:26:25,928
Their eyelashes are long and thick, in two rows, to keep out the blowing sand, and they can close their nostrils almost completely, down to narrow slits, when the wind carries dust.

212
00:26:25,928 --> 00:26:30,738
Their coats are thick and woolly, already growing in for the winter.

213
00:26:30,738 --> 00:26:38,236
In spring they will shed great soft clumps of it, and the drivers will gather it up and spin it into yarn.

214
00:26:39,736 --> 00:26:41,893
Not every load is taken off.

215
00:26:41,893 --> 00:26:45,285
This is only a rest, not a camp for the day.

216
00:26:45,285 --> 00:26:57,847
But the frames are loosened a little, so the camels can breathe more easily, and some of the heavier bundles are lifted down and stacked in a low wall on the sand.

217
00:26:57,847 --> 00:27:04,013
The animals stretch their long necks out along the ground, and close their eyes.

218
00:27:05,513 --> 00:27:19,749
Around the edge of the well, someone finds a few dry tamarisk branches, and in a few minutes there is a small fire burning, low and bright, with an iron pot balanced on three stones above it.

219
00:27:19,749 --> 00:27:29,662
The smell of woodsmoke drifts across the hollow, and the travellers gather around the warmth, wrapped in their coats and felt cloaks.

220
00:27:31,162 --> 00:27:38,137
There is warm millet porridge, a little salty, with a handful of dried apricots stirred in.

221
00:27:38,137 --> 00:27:43,809
There is flatbread, warmed at the edge of the fire until it is soft again.

222
00:27:43,809 --> 00:27:52,163
There is a piece of hard cheese, passed from hand to hand, each person cutting off a thin slice with a knife.

223
00:27:52,163 --> 00:27:53,850
Nobody says very much.

224
00:27:53,850 --> 00:27:56,762
They are tired, and warm, and content.

225
00:27:58,262 --> 00:28:02,020
Above the hollow, the sky is still full of stars.

226
00:28:02,020 --> 00:28:05,931
Below them, the dunes are black against the silver.

227
00:28:05,931 --> 00:28:20,961
And between the dunes and the stars, there is this one small circle of firelight, with tired people and kneeling camels around it, and the quiet, steady sound of water dripping back into the well.

228
00:28:30,461 --> 00:28:35,982
After the meal, one of the drivers unwraps the lute from its padded cloth.

229
00:28:35,982 --> 00:28:44,936
It is a pipa, with a pear-shaped body of polished wood and four silk strings, and its neck bent sharply back at the top.

230
00:28:44,936 --> 00:28:59,709
Instruments like this came east along the Silk Road from Central Asia, and by the time of our story they were loved everywhere in China, in palaces and in taverns and around campfires like this one.

231
00:29:01,209 --> 00:29:08,058
He tunes it slowly, turning the pegs, plucking each string, listening, and turning again.

232
00:29:08,058 --> 00:29:09,828
Then he begins to play.

233
00:29:09,828 --> 00:29:17,984
Not a lively dance tune, but something slow and wandering, a melody from his home valley, far to the west.

234
00:29:17,984 --> 00:29:24,910
The notes are soft and round, and they drift out over the hollow and fade among the dunes.

235
00:29:26,410 --> 00:29:30,415
The Sogdians were famous for their music and dancing.

236
00:29:30,415 --> 00:29:46,810
In the great cities of China, people crowded to watch Sogdian dancers perform a whirling dance, spinning so fast on a small round carpet that their sleeves and ribbons flew out around them like the petals of a flower.

237
00:29:46,810 --> 00:29:48,397
Poets wrote about it.

238
00:29:48,397 --> 00:29:49,908
Emperors watched it.

239
00:29:49,908 --> 00:29:52,326
But tonight there is no dancing.

240
00:29:52,326 --> 00:29:55,424
Tonight, the music is only for listening.

241
00:29:56,924 --> 00:30:02,217
The old merchant hums along under his breath, his eyes half closed.

242
00:30:02,217 --> 00:30:11,461
The monk sits a little apart, on a folded cloak, his hands resting in his lap, listening with a quiet, distant smile.

243
00:30:11,461 --> 00:30:21,180
The boy who tied the bells is lying on his back on the sand, looking straight up at the stars, his breathing slow and deep.

244
00:30:22,680 --> 00:30:25,244
When the song ends, nobody claps.

245
00:30:25,244 --> 00:30:32,550
The player lets the last note fade, and then rests his hand flat on the strings to quiet them.

246
00:30:32,550 --> 00:30:43,586
For a while there is only the sound of the fire, and the camels breathing, and the faint whisper of sand moving somewhere out in the darkness.

247
00:30:45,086 --> 00:30:49,749
Then the old merchant begins to tell a story, in a low voice.

248
00:30:49,749 --> 00:31:00,987
It is a story about a merchant who travelled so far west that he came to a city made entirely of white stone, beside a sea that had no other shore.

249
00:31:00,987 --> 00:31:06,720
Whether he means a real city, or only a city from an old tale, nobody asks.

250
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:08,784
It does not seem to matter.

251
00:31:08,784 --> 00:31:13,524
The story wanders, like the music did, and like the road does.

252
00:31:15,024 --> 00:31:16,698
The fire burns lower.

253
00:31:16,698 --> 00:31:24,352
The red embers settle, and glow, and fade a little, and glow again when the breeze touches them.

254
00:31:24,352 --> 00:31:28,178
The iron pot is cool now, set aside on the sand.

255
00:31:28,178 --> 00:31:39,898
Some of the drivers have wrapped themselves in their cloaks and lain down beside their camels, using the soft warm flank of the animal as a pillow.

256
00:31:41,398 --> 00:31:46,088
High above, the Silver River has turned a little in the sky.

257
00:31:46,088 --> 00:31:51,091
The moon has begun its long slope down toward the western dunes.

258
00:31:51,091 --> 00:31:58,049
The leader looks up at it, and then at the fire, and then at the sleepy faces around him.

259
00:31:58,049 --> 00:32:00,550
There is still time, he decides.

260
00:32:00,550 --> 00:32:02,036
A little more rest.

261
00:32:02,036 --> 00:32:03,521
The road will wait.

262
00:32:13,021 --> 00:32:18,804
At last, in the deepest part of the night, the caravan moves on once more.

263
00:32:18,804 --> 00:32:26,775
The camels rise, groaning softly, and the loads are tightened, and the fire is covered over with sand.

264
00:32:26,775 --> 00:32:35,528
The bell at the front gives its first slow note, and the long line stretches out again across the moonlit dunes.

265
00:32:37,028 --> 00:32:47,020
Now the moon is low in the west, and the shadows of the camels have grown very long, reaching out ahead of them across the sand.

266
00:32:47,020 --> 00:32:49,050
The air is at its coolest.

267
00:32:49,050 --> 00:32:58,809
The travellers pull their coats tighter, and settle deeper into their saddles, and let the camels choose their own slow pace.

268
00:33:00,309 --> 00:33:02,820
Hardly anyone is fully awake now.

269
00:33:02,820 --> 00:33:09,821
The leader, of course, walking at the front, with one hand resting on the lead camel's rope.

270
00:33:09,821 --> 00:33:15,451
And perhaps one of the drivers at the back, listening for the second bell.

271
00:33:15,451 --> 00:33:24,202
Everyone else is drifting, half asleep, rocked gently by the long, swaying walk, through the cool and endless dark.

272
00:33:25,702 --> 00:33:34,379
The track winds on between the dunes, up a long slope of soft sand, along a ridge, and gently down again.

273
00:33:34,379 --> 00:33:35,702
The bell sounds.

274
00:33:35,702 --> 00:33:37,272
The camels breathe.

275
00:33:37,272 --> 00:33:40,412
The stars turn, very slowly, overhead.

276
00:33:40,412 --> 00:33:42,395
Up, and along, and down.

277
00:33:42,395 --> 00:33:44,379
Up, and along, and down.

278
00:33:44,379 --> 00:33:48,593
There is nothing to watch for, nothing to remember.

279
00:33:48,593 --> 00:33:50,577
Only the road, going on.

280
00:33:52,077 --> 00:33:55,693
Far behind them now, the oasis town is sleeping.

281
00:33:55,693 --> 00:34:00,214
The market stalls are empty, and the poplar trees are still.

282
00:34:00,214 --> 00:34:09,331
In the cliff of caves, the monks have put out their lamps, and the painted musicians and dancers rest unseen in the dark.

283
00:34:09,331 --> 00:34:19,277
Beside the crescent spring, between the great sand hills, the reeds stand perfectly still, and the water holds the last of the moon.

284
00:34:20,777 --> 00:34:24,347
Far ahead of them, other towns are sleeping too.

285
00:34:24,347 --> 00:34:26,430
Turfan, among its vineyards.

286
00:34:26,430 --> 00:34:29,554
Kucha, with its music and its monasteries.

287
00:34:29,554 --> 00:34:45,993
And far, far away, beyond the mountains, Samarkand, with its orchards and its blue evening, where some of these merchants have families who are thinking of them tonight, and wondering where on the long road they might be.

288
00:34:47,493 --> 00:34:58,220
And all along the road between, in hollows and beside wells and in the shelter of old watchtowers, there are other caravans like this one.

289
00:34:58,220 --> 00:34:59,231
Some resting.

290
00:34:59,231 --> 00:35:02,029
Some moving slowly through the dark.

291
00:35:02,029 --> 00:35:14,778
Each one a small line of bells and footsteps, crossing the great silent space of the desert, carrying silk and paper, stories and songs, from one oasis to the next.

292
00:35:16,278 --> 00:35:22,094
In the east, behind the caravan, the sky is just beginning to change.

293
00:35:22,094 --> 00:35:23,780
It is not light yet.

294
00:35:23,780 --> 00:35:31,871
Only a faint softening of the darkness along the horizon, a hint of grey, and then of pale blue.

295
00:35:31,871 --> 00:35:35,074
The brightest stars are still shining.

296
00:35:35,074 --> 00:35:38,614
The others are quietly fading, one by one.

297
00:35:40,114 --> 00:35:47,113
Soon the caravan will stop for the day, in the shade of a rocky outcrop the leader knows well.

298
00:35:47,113 --> 00:35:58,728
The camels will kneel, and the loads will come down, and the travellers will sleep through the warm hours, under awnings of felt, until evening comes again.

299
00:35:58,728 --> 00:36:00,887
But that is tomorrow's story.

300
00:36:00,887 --> 00:36:02,972
For now, there is only this.

301
00:36:02,972 --> 00:36:06,546
The soft sand, the slow bell, the turning stars.

302
00:36:08,046 --> 00:36:11,723
You can let the caravan go on without you now.

303
00:36:11,723 --> 00:36:14,521
You have walked far enough tonight.

304
00:36:14,521 --> 00:36:23,634
Let the sound of the bell grow fainter, and fainter, as the long line of camels moves away over the moonlit dunes.

305
00:36:23,634 --> 00:36:28,270
Let the desert grow quiet, and wide, and still around you.

306
00:36:29,770 --> 00:36:34,248
Thank you for travelling with The Quiet Archive tonight.

307
00:36:34,248 --> 00:36:38,246
Rest easy, wherever your own road has brought you.

308
00:36:38,246 --> 00:36:39,125
Sleep well.

309
00:36:39,125 --> 00:36:40,245
And goodnight.
